nd rerun each failure with the same seed and build to rule out flakiness. Neither has been done in the cited evidence."}], "files": [{"path": "tb/tb_pwm_pulse_width.v", "kind": "testbench", "title": "Repro B1: pulse width equals duty", "summary": "Self-checking repro for bucket B1 (pulse_width) of [requirement:req-e946df186d], written against pwm_gen in [artifact:prj-f58c6d66a9.design-under-investigation.a1]. With duty held at 3, then 5, then 1, it measures steady-state pwm high-run lengths and expects each to equal duty. The `cnt <= duty` compare gives duty+1. The check counts only the high run, so it does not depend on the period length or on when duty is sampled. It ends with one NIRMAAN-SIM verdict carrying the run's nonce.", "entry": "tb_pwm_pulse_width"}, {"path": "tb/tb_pwm_period.v", "kind": "testbench", "title": "Repro B2: period equals PERIOD", "summary": "Self-checking repro for bucket B2 (period) of [requirement:req-e946df186d], written against pwm_gen in [artifact:prj-f58c6d66a9.design-under-investigation.a1]. With duty held at 3, then 5, it measures the gap between successive pwm rising edges and expects PERIOD (8). The wrap at PERIOD-2 gives 7. The rise-to-rise gap does not depend on the high-run width. It ends with one NIRMAAN-SIM verdict carrying the run's nonce.", "entry": "tb_pwm_period"}, {"path": "tb/tb_pwm_duty_update.v", "kind": "testbench", "title": "Repro B3: duty change takes effect next period", "summary": "Self-checking repro for bucket B3 (duty_update) of [requirement:req-e946df186d], written against pwm_gen in [artifact:prj-f58c6d66a9.design-under-investigation.a1]. It raises duty from 2 to 6 while pwm is low mid-period, and expects the next high run to equal the following steady run. It then lowers duty from 6 to 2 during a pulse, and expects that pulse to keep its old length. The live-duty compare breaks both checks. Lengths are compared with each other, not with constants, so width and period faults are not counted here. It ends with